<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Pablo Fonseca</title><link>https://pablobfonseca.github.io/</link><description>Recent content on Pablo Fonseca</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Mon, 01 Apr 2019 15:05:01 -0300</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://pablobfonseca.github.io/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>JS Reference vs Copy</title><link>https://pablobfonseca.github.io/posts/js-reference-vs-copy/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Apr 2019 15:05:01 -0300</pubDate><guid>https://pablobfonseca.github.io/posts/js-reference-vs-copy/</guid><description>&lt;p>It&amp;rsquo;s been a while since I don&amp;rsquo;t write anything here, but here we go :)&lt;/p>
&lt;p>I have been studying a lot of JS lately, and I bumped into some real nice tips,
it can be simple for those who already know JS but for me it was new and really
cool.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>In Javascript if you assign the value of a variable to another variable like
this:&lt;/p>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">2
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">3
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">4
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">5
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-javascript" data-lang="javascript">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="kd">let&lt;/span> &lt;span class="nx">name&lt;/span> &lt;span class="o">=&lt;/span> &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;Pablo&amp;#34;&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">;&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="kd">let&lt;/span> &lt;span class="nx">name2&lt;/span> &lt;span class="o">=&lt;/span> &lt;span class="nx">name&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">;&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="nx">name2&lt;/span> &lt;span class="o">=&lt;/span> &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;Peter&amp;#34;&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">;&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="nx">console&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">.&lt;/span>&lt;span class="nx">log&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">(&lt;/span>&lt;span class="nx">name2&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">);&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>This code above will return &amp;lsquo;Pablo&amp;rsquo; or &amp;lsquo;Peter&amp;rsquo;? It will return &amp;lsquo;Peter&amp;rsquo; because
we re-assign the value of the variable &lt;code>name2&lt;/code>.&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>Ruby - Introduction to ruby objects</title><link>https://pablobfonseca.github.io/posts/ruby-basics-introduction-to-objects/</link><pubDate>Wed, 07 Sep 2016 19:10:00 -0300</pubDate><guid>https://pablobfonseca.github.io/posts/ruby-basics-introduction-to-objects/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="intro">
 Intro
 &lt;a class="heading-link" href="#intro">
 &lt;i class="fa-solid fa-link" aria-hidden="true" title="Link to heading">&lt;/i>
 &lt;span class="sr-only">Link to heading&lt;/span>
 &lt;/a>
&lt;/h2>
&lt;p>We&amp;rsquo;re going to go through the basics of Ruby objects in these series of articles and try to cover all you need to know to get started with Ruby.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>Remeber that you can test the highlighted codes directly from your terminal
opening the IRB (Interactive Ruby Shell), to do so, just type this in yout
terminal:&lt;/p>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-fallback" data-lang="fallback">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">irb
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>So, let&amp;rsquo;s get started!&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>Make non ActiveRecord classes renderable</title><link>https://pablobfonseca.github.io/posts/make-non-activerecord-renderable/</link><pubDate>Mon, 22 Aug 2016 18:00:00 -0300</pubDate><guid>https://pablobfonseca.github.io/posts/make-non-activerecord-renderable/</guid><description>&lt;p>In order to render a new partial without being a &amp;ldquo;Rails&amp;rdquo; object we can rewrite the method &lt;code>to_partial_path&lt;/code> to do so:&lt;/p>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">2
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">3
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">4
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">5
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-ruby" data-lang="ruby">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="k">class&lt;/span> &lt;span class="nc">Timeline&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="k">def&lt;/span> &lt;span class="nf">to_partial_path&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;timeline/timeline&amp;#34;&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="k">end&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="k">end&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>Now we just need to create the view in &lt;code>app/views/timeline/_timeline.htm.erb&lt;/code> and it&amp;rsquo;s going to work like a charm!&lt;/p>
&lt;p>or, we can extend the &lt;code>ActiveModel::Naming&lt;/code> to make it works as well:&lt;/p>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">2
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">3
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-ruby" data-lang="ruby">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="k">class&lt;/span> &lt;span class="nc">Timeline&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="kp">extend&lt;/span> &lt;span class="no">ActiveModel&lt;/span>&lt;span class="o">::&lt;/span>&lt;span class="no">Naming&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="k">end&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>&lt;q>Happy coding!&lt;/q>&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>Underscore - Ruby Secret Weapon</title><link>https://pablobfonseca.github.io/posts/underscore-ruby-secret-weapon/</link><pubDate>Mon, 18 Jul 2016 18:40:00 -0300</pubDate><guid>https://pablobfonseca.github.io/posts/underscore-ruby-secret-weapon/</guid><description>&lt;p>Once in IRB, the underscore holds the return value from the last command.&lt;/p>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">2
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">3
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">4
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-ruby" data-lang="ruby">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="p">{&lt;/span>&lt;span class="nb">name&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">:&lt;/span> &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;John Doe&amp;#34;&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span> &lt;span class="ss">job&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">:&lt;/span> &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;Developer&amp;#34;&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">}&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="n">data&lt;/span> &lt;span class="o">=&lt;/span> &lt;span class="n">_&lt;/span> &lt;span class="c1"># it assigns the last command to the variable data&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="n">data&lt;/span>&lt;span class="o">.&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">inspect&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># =&amp;gt; {name: &amp;#34;John Doe&amp;#34;, job: &amp;#34;Developer&amp;#34;}&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>Guess what? It works in the Rails console too :)&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>Welcome to my blog</title><link>https://pablobfonseca.github.io/posts/welcome-to-my-blog/</link><pubDate>Sun, 17 Jul 2016 15:49:01 -0300</pubDate><guid>https://pablobfonseca.github.io/posts/welcome-to-my-blog/</guid><description>&lt;p>Welcome, this blog is a place to share information about web development,
